{:edb/db-before-t 1277 :edb/db-after-t 1278 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534590 50 #inst "2026-10-08T01:50:00.595Z" 13194139534590 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297497 13194139534590 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297516 13194139534590 true] [17592186157374 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297523 13194139534590 true] [17592186157375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297504 13194139534590 true] [17592186157375 1020 #edb/ref 17592186297511 13194139534590 true] [17592186297497 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297498 13194139534590 true] [17592186297497 1089 "q-115053" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297497 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534590 true] [17592186297497 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{9-2x}-3>-4.$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297497 1113 #uuid "db7eb34c-19dc-5bac-aefb-fd226ead4846" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297497 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{9-2x}-3 & >-4 \\\\ \\sqrt{9-2x} & >-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-1),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9-2x & \\ge 0 \\\\ -2x & \\ge -9 \\\\ x & \\le \\frac{9}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\le \\frac{9}{2}.$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297499 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297500 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297501 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297502 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297503 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1041 "selection" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297503 13194139534590 true] [17592186297498 1110 #uuid "3d1bb207-997d-50fb-b711-479caefb0a09" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297499 1034 "x\\le -4" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297499 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297499 1111 #uuid "1a4b9d89-3ff5-54da-820b-eb021d5fe3e0" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297500 1034 "x\\ge -1" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297500 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297500 1111 #uuid "42a00f97-17d6-5781-b3c2-c517e6a2a502" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297501 1034 "x\\le 1" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297501 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297501 1111 #uuid "b0db2e2f-dd51-5285-bae9-7714789b537f" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297502 1034 "x\\ge \\frac{3}{4}" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297502 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297502 1111 #uuid "05648092-eeca-52af-ad86-0c572122b53f" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297503 1034 "x\\le \\frac{9}{2}" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297503 1111 #uuid "d457b80a-0685-5505-9493-6872f3eabf07" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297504 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297505 13194139534590 true] [17592186297504 1089 "q-115060" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297504 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534590 true] [17592186297504 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{x+2}-4>-2.$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297504 1113 #uuid "3e85dd46-e7c3-5897-aad1-f61ca46bc9a9" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297504 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{x+2}-4 & >-2 \\\\ \\sqrt{x+2} & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(2).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{x+2})}^{2} & >{2}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & >4 \\\\ x & >2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & \\ge 0 \\\\ x & \\ge -2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x>2,$ which can be expressed as $(2,\\infty ).$ The domain of the square root is $x\\ge -2,$ which can be expressed as $[-2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n(2,\\infty )\\cap [-2,\\infty )=(2,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in (2,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $x>2.$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297506 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297507 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297508 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297509 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297510 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1041 "selection" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297506 13194139534590 true] [17592186297505 1110 #uuid "6c99caff-e9b9-51cb-9451-e28781324705" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297506 1034 "x>2" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297506 1111 #uuid "c8691714-11bf-5d6f-b408-dd725905dd83" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297507 1034 "-2<x\\le 4" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297507 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297507 1111 #uuid "b1e5f26a-8b86-5d1b-9905-5b8a2be8b11b" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297508 1034 "x<4" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297508 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297508 1111 #uuid "0bdc5ff1-eadc-511f-868d-7fbf1f58a122" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297509 1034 "-4\\le x<2" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297509 1111 #uuid "24e9e9cb-b2b4-563e-9932-cfa325a61465" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297510 1034 "x\\ge 2" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297510 1111 #uuid "3ba67780-950f-5ca0-b6fe-c6874b19b240" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297511 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297512 13194139534590 true] [17592186297511 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297514 13194139534590 true] [17592186297511 1089 "q-278283" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297511 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534590 true] [17592186297511 1100 "Solve the inequality $\\sqrt{3x-6}-4\\ge -1.$\n\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\n$x$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297511 1113 #uuid "f2f0b8e2-445d-5900-a2e9-e7c3538ed57d" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297511 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{3x-6}-4 & \\ge -1 \\\\ \\sqrt{3x-6} & \\ge 3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this inequality, the square root is being compared to a non-negative number $(3).$ So, we can square both sides of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\sqrt{3x-6})}^{2} & \\ge {3}^{2} \\\\ 3x-6 & \\ge 9 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 15 \\\\ x & \\ge 5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we must only keep the solutions that are in the domain of the square root. To find the domain of the square root, we recall that the argument of the square root must be greater than or equal to zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-6 & \\ge 0 \\\\ 3x & \\ge 6 \\\\ x & \\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOur solution is $x\\ge 5,$ which can be expressed as $[5,\\infty ).$ But the domain of the square root is $x\\ge 2,$ which can be expressed as $[2,\\infty ).$ To find the solutions that are in the domain of the square root, we take the intersection:\n\n$$\n[5,\\infty )\\cap [2,\\infty )=[5,\\infty )\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\in [5,\\infty ),$ which can also be written as $5\\le x.$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297512 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297513 13194139534590 true] [17592186297512 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534590 true] [17592186297512 1041 "field-1" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297512 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297513 13194139534590 true] [17592186297512 1110 #uuid "45b7203a-834c-5df9-b9e7-443f4f3330cc" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297513 1034 "5" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297513 1111 #uuid "3e544178-ab30-5a9a-ae0a-a927f900d259" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297514 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297515 13194139534590 true] [17592186297514 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534590 true] [17592186297514 1041 "field-2" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297514 1104 #edb/ref 17592186297515 13194139534590 true] [17592186297514 1110 #uuid "ff6e0618-95f6-54cc-8fba-b44f8e952f66" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297515 1034 "\\le" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297515 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534590 true] [17592186297515 1111 #uuid "f56d7949-c7ac-5bc6-8fee-6b5d103a6a80" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297516 1017 #edb/ref 17592186297517 13194139534590 true] [17592186297516 1089 "q-87681" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297516 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139534590 true] [17592186297516 1100 "Solve the inequality $3-\\sqrt{x-1}<8.$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297516 1113 #uuid "5e440b43-c841-53b8-a07a-d961fcc2ab24" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297516 1145 "First, we isolate the square root:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3-\\sqrt{x-1} & <8 \\\\ -\\sqrt{x-1} & <5 \\\\ \\sqrt{x-1} & >-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that we had to flip the sign of the inequality since we divided by a negative number.\n\nHere, the square root is greater than a negative number $(-5),$ so any number we can substitute into the square root will satisfy the inequality. So, the solution is the domain of the square root.\n\nTo find the domain, we recall that the expression under the square root must be greater than or equal to $0:$\n\n$$\nx-1\\ge 0\\,⇒\\,x\\ge 1\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x\\ge 1.$" 13194139534590 true] [17592186297517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297518 13194139534590 true] [17592186297517 1000 #edb/ref 17592186297519 13194139534590 true] [17592186297517 1000 #ed